WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.070
Follow Langfocus on Facebook Twitter and

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.790
Instagram

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:06.819
Hello everyone, welcome to the Langfocus

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:08.740
channel and my name is Paul. Today's

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.750
video is a quick video to explain a

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.519
concept that I often talk about in my

00:00:12.519 --> 00:00:14.320
videos but the viewers sometimes don't

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.090
understand and they asked me about in

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:16.990
the comments.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:19.630
The concept is grammatical case. Case

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.700
refers to the grammatical function of a

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.890
noun or a pronoun in a sentence. For

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.810
example if these are all the words in a

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:26.470
sentence:

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.619
ate lion honey badger the a

00:00:29.619 --> 00:00:32.380
there two possible messages: this one

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.690
"The lion ate the honey badger," or this one:

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:37.660
"The honey badger ate the lion." There are

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.430
two roles in the sentence: the animal

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.290
doing the eating, and the animal being

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.120
eaten. In English we normally know which

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.220
role a noun is fulfilling based on word

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.290
order. The subject of the sentence comes

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.480
*here* and the object of a sentence comes

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:50.320
*here*

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.360
Prepositions can also tell us a noun's function

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.430
for example "The honey badger

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.140
gave a warning to the snake." The

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.480
preposition "to" tells us the snake is the

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.230
indirect object, receiving the action. But

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.820
in many languages word order and

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.619
prepositions are not the main ways to

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:07.420
show the function of a noun. The form of

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:09.520
the noun might change or a special ending

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.260
might be added to the word to show its

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.940
function in the sentence. In English we

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.860
do this to show possession like in the

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.680
phrase "The lion's defeat." The 's

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.870
shows that the defeat belongs to the

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.790
lion. And in English we also have

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.650
pronouns that change form to show

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.110
grammatical case. For example "I robbed

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:27.760
her.  / She robbed me."

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.280
"I" and "she" are the subject forms while "me"

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.740
and "her" are the object forms. But there

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.360
are languages that show grammatical case

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.250
much more than English does. The most

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.049
common grammatical cases are probably

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:40.270
nominative, accusative, and genitive.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.640
Nominative case generally indicates the

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.710
subject of a sentence, which often means

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:46.630
the person or thing doing the action.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:50.049
Like this in Arabic:  Akala al waladu tufaaHatan

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:52.869
meaning "The boy ate an apple." Here we can

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:55.299
see that the word for boy - "walad" - has an

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:57.670
"u" at the end because it is the subject

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:59.500
of the sentence. And nominative case can

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.689
also indicate the thing that the subject

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:04.180
is equivalent to so in this sentence: "Hua

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:05.830
tilmithun" here the word for

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:08.259
student "tilmith" has "un" at the end to

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:10.299
indicate nominative case because it is

00:02:10.299 --> 00:02:12.519
equal to the subject. This ending has the

00:02:12.519 --> 00:02:14.859
extra "n" sound at the end because it's

00:02:14.859 --> 00:02:17.110
indefinite. These kinds of changes

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:18.850
the form of a word to show grammatical

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:20.470
difference are called inflections.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:22.780
Accusative case generally indicates the

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.360
direct object of a verb. In Arabic it has

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.250
that use along with some other uses. For

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:30.280
example "Ashrabu al-Haliiba" which means "I

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.070
drink milk." Here the word for milk "Haliib"

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:35.620
has an "a" at the end to indicate that

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.900
it's the direct object or the accusative

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.390
case. Genitive case generally indicates

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:42.310
that one noun is being modified by

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:43.959
another one often to show possession.

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:47.590
"Mudarrisu l-waladi" meaning "The boy's teacher."

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.560
Here the word for boy "walad" has an "i"

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.750
sound at the end to show possession.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:54.820
Those are probably the three cases

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.650
you're most likely to run into but

00:02:56.650 --> 00:02:58.000
depending on the language there are

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.280
numerous other cases as well. The dative

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.410
case is generally used for a noun which

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.120
receives something, something moves

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.620
towards that noun.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:09.340
"Pecuniam puellae non dedii" -  "I didn't give the

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.830
girl money." Here the Latin word for girl

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:14.860
"puella" has an "e" at the end to indicate

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.840
that she is the indirect object, on the

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:20.110
receiving end of an action. Locative case is

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:22.000
used to indicate a location.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.980
Here's an example from Turkish. "Evde

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.709
bir kitap okudum" which means "I read a

00:03:26.709 --> 00:03:28.989
book at home." Here the word for house

00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:31.540
"ev" has "de" at the end to show that it's

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:33.160
the location where the reading took

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.410
place. In English we use a separate

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:37.930
preposition like "at" "in" or "on" to show

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:39.910
location, rather than a case inflection.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.160
Instrumental case is used for a noun

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.080
that is used as a tool or a means of

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.519
completing an action.

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:47.560
Here's an example from Polish "Piszę piórem"

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:50.620
which means "I write with a pen." The

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:52.989
word for pen "pióro" ends with a

00:03:52.989 --> 00:03:55.420
final "o" which is replaced by "em"

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:57.700
because it's the instrument being used

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.260
to carry out the action of writing.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.390
Notice that because of that ending, there

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:02.920
is no need to add a standalone

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:05.170
preposition meaning "with". Those are some

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.000
of the most common cases. The main thing

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.650
to keep in mind is that the form of the

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.900
noun or of the pronoun changes depending

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:12.430
on its function in the sentence.

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:14.739
Sometimes cases apply not only to

00:04:14.739 --> 00:04:16.419
nouns but also to words that are

00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:18.250
connected to them like adjectives and

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:18.970
articles.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:20.739
Here's an example of an adjective being

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:22.990
inflected for case, this time again in

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.479
Arabic. "Al-bintu al-jamiilatu" which

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:27.880
means "the beautiful girl." Here the noun

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:30.460
for girl "bint" has the nominative case

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.229
ending and so does the adjective

00:04:32.229 --> 00:04:35.229
"jamillah." But the Arab equivalent of "h" at

00:04:35.229 --> 00:04:36.970
the end of the world changes to a "t"

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:39.669
sound before the case ending. And here's

00:04:39.669 --> 00:04:41.560
an example of articles taking case

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.180
inflection, this time from Old English.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.840
"Se cyning" - "the king" in nominative case. In

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.490
the genitive case it's "þǽs cyninges"

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:50.500
so the noun is inflicted with the

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.150
genitive ending and the definite article

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:54.460
also has a special form in the genitive

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.610
case. Words have particular functions in

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.349
all languages, of course. But only in

00:04:59.349 --> 00:05:00.970
certain languages do they commonly

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:03.400
indicate these functions with changes to

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.199
the form of the word. When talking about

00:05:05.199 --> 00:05:07.270
languages that do so we often talk about

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.000
noun cases or case endings. But remember

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.190
that not only nouns can be inflicted for

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.830
case but also pronouns, which represent

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:17.169
nouns, and also adjectives, which modify

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:19.419
nouns, and articles, which show whether

00:05:19.419 --> 00:05:21.909
nouns are definite or indefinite. I hope

00:05:21.909 --> 00:05:23.680
that was helpful summary for anyone who

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:25.630
needed clarification. So the quick

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.520
Question of the Day: Do words in your

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.710
language change form depending on their

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:31.330
function in the sentence? If you can

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:32.889
think of an example, let us know in the

00:05:32.889 --> 00:05:34.690
comments down below." Be sure to follow

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:36.460
Langfocus on Facebook, Twitter and

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.440
Instagram, and once again thanks to all

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:40.270
of my Patreon supporters, especially

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:42.250
these extra special ones right here, for

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:44.139
their monthly pledges. Thank you for

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:56.710
watching and have a nice day.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:03.380
Oh, right, and Happy New Year!

